<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arturo Araujo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arturo Araujo was a Salvadoran landowner and engineer who won the free presidential election of 1931 with the support of labour and peasant groups. He took office in March 1931, but his government was overwhelmed by the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> prices in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. In December 1931 a military coup removed him and brought his vice-president, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a>, to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo.jpg" alt="Arturo Araujo" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arturo_Araujo_Fajardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
